  "slug": "untitled-topic-876d",
  "summary": "Recent preprints address specific error sources and resource bottlenecks in structured light, single-pixel, ptychographic and hyperspectral imaging.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the recovery of optical field information from constrained or imperfect measurements, achieved through computation or engineered illumination and readout. Active convolved illumination (arXiv:2611.17471) is reviewed as a framework for preserving optical mode structure through turbulent media via segmented reciprocal-space control. Within single-pixel imaging, a Fourier-domain response-matrix correction (arXiv:2611.19283) addresses quantization errors in binary-encoded Fourier acquisition, whereas arXiv:2611.25700 converts temporal mixing into a measurement resource through a unified calibration and composite-readout framework. A stochastic gradient descent framework for X-ray ptychographic reconstruction (arXiv:2611.24232) reports peak GPU memory reduced to 65% and time to 22% relative to the comparison method. Event-camera Fourier-transform hyperspectral microscopy (arXiv:2611.26097) reaches real-time widefield spectral acquisition. The modalities differ; the novelty lies in targeted treatment of specific error sources or resource bottlenecks within each.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
